<h1>Security</h1>

<p>This document covers Pilot Protocol's security posture, including cryptography, trust model, data transparency, audit, and vulnerability reporting.</p>

<h2>Cryptography</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Identity — every node holds a persistent Ed25519 keypair (`~/.pilot/identity.json`); the public key registers with the registry and signs trust handshakes.</li>
  <li>Tunnels — X25519 key exchange, AES-256-GCM per tunnel, over UDP (or a single outbound TLS/443 connection in compat mode).</li>
  <li>Implementation — Go standard library only; no external crypto dependencies. The wire format is specified in an IETF Internet-Draft.</li>
</ul>
<p>Further details are available in Core Concepts.</p>

<h2>Trust model</h2>
<p>Connections require a mutual handshake where both sides approve before traffic flows. Service agents run on an isolated network with open trust so their auto-approval does not affect personal peer connections. The source code is open source under AGPL-3.0 and available at github.com/pilot-protocol.</p>

<h2>Data &amp; consent transparency</h2>
<p>Pilot has four default-on features, each disclosed by the installer and individually disableable: app-store telemetry, network broadcasts, review prompts, and skill injection. Skill injection writes a Pilot skill into detected agent toolchains such as Claude Code, Cursor, OpenClaw, OpenHands, and Hermes. The `pilotctl skills status` command previews every pending change. Message payloads to specialists are end-to-end encrypted in P2P mode, with no third party in the path.</p>
<p>The Consent &amp; Privacy document details each default, the exact files written, the threat model, the cost of disabling each, and the one-line opt-outs.</p>

<h2>Enterprise controls</h2>
<p>RBAC, SSO / identity integration, network policies, and audit export (Splunk HEC, CEF/Syslog, JSON) are covered in the Enterprise section. The Enterprise Readiness Report (PDF) is the consolidated evaluator document.</p>

<h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
<p>Email founders@pilotprotocol.network. A machine-readable contact is available at `/.well-known/security.txt`.</p>

